.TH "YAESM" "1" "" "yaesm 0.0.3"
.SH NAME
yaesm \- a configurable backup system that connects backup tools into
pipelines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
yaesm \-\-help
yaesm \-\-version
yaesm [OPTIONS] SUBCOMMAND [SUBCOMMAND_OPTIONS]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Yaesm is a configurable backup system.
A single configuration file defines the backups it runs and how they
behave.
.PP
Each backup is a pipeline assembled from components.
It reads data from a source, may compress, encrypt, or otherwise
transform it, and stores the result at a destination.
Components may run locally or through OpenSSH, enabling local backups,
remote transfers, and replication of existing backups.
.PP
Yaesm runs as a scheduler daemon, executing backups according to their
schedules and accepting on\-demand requests through a local control
socket.
A backup may have multiple schedules, each with its own retention
policy.
Related backups can be collected into groups and operated on together.
Its command\-line subcommands include operations for starting and
interacting with the daemon, checking the configuration, and finding
stored backups.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file uses YAML 1.1 and is organized around three kinds
of top\-level entries: one or more backup definitions, optional group
definitions, and an optional \f[CR]settings\fR section.
Each backup definition describes a pipeline, each group definition
collects backups and other groups under a single name, and the
\f[CR]settings\fR section controls behavior shared across backups.
.SS EXAMPLE CONFIGURATION
The example below shows how the pieces of a yaesm configuration fit
together.
It deliberately includes several optional features; a basic
configuration can be much shorter, and each part is fully explained in
the following sections.
.IP
.EX
settings\fB:\fR
  ssh\fB:\fR
    identity_file\fB:\fR /root/.ssh/id_ed25519
    config_file\fB:\fR /root/.ssh/config
  scheduler\fB:\fR
    timezone\fB:\fR America/New_York
    max_concurrent_backups\fB:\fR 2

primary\-backups\fB:\fR
  group\fB:\fR
    \fB\-\fR encrypted\-home
    \fB\-\fR root\-snapshots

encrypted\-home\fB:\fR
  previous_names\fB:\fR
    \fB\-\fR home\-archive
  ssh\fB:\fR
    endpoint\fB:\fR ssh://server.example.com
  source\fB:\fR
    directory\fB:\fR /home
    remote\fB:\fR true
  transforms\fB:\fR
    \fB\-\fR tar\fB:\fR \fB{}\fR
      remote\fB:\fR true
    \fB\-\fR zstd\fB:\fR 3
      remote\fB:\fR true
    \fB\-\fR gpg\fB:\fR /etc/yaesm/backup\-key.asc
      remote\fB:\fR true
  destination\fB:\fR
    file\fB:\fR /srv/backups
  schedules\fB:\fR
    hourly\fB:\fR
      previous_names\fB:\fR
        \fB\-\fR frequent
      cron\fB:\fR \(dq0 * * * *\(dq
      retention\fB:\fR
        \fB\-\fR keep\-last\fB:\fR 24
        \fB\-\fR keep\-for\fB:\fR 7d
    manual\fB:\fR
      on\-demand\fB:\fR \fB{}\fR
      retention\fB:\fR
        keep\-all\fB:\fR \fB{}\fR

offsite\-copy\fB:\fR
  skip_unchanged\fB:\fR true
  ssh\fB:\fR
    endpoint\fB:\fR ssh://backup.example.com
  source\fB:\fR
    backup\fB:\fR encrypted\-home
  destination\fB:\fR
    file\fB:\fR /srv/backups
    remote\fB:\fR true

root\-snapshots\fB:\fR
  source\fB:\fR
    btrfs\fB:\fR /
  destination\fB:\fR
    btrfs\fB:\fR /.snapshots/yaesm
.EE
.SS BACKUP DEFINITIONS
Backup definitions are the core of yaesm\(aqs configuration: they
determine what data is backed up, how it is processed, and where it is
stored.
Every top\-level entry other than \f[CR]settings\fR and group
definitions is treated as a backup definition.
The entry\(aqs key is the backup name, and its value is a mapping that
defines the backup pipeline and its behavior.
.PP
Backup definitions share a common structure, while the
\f[CR]source\fR, \f[CR]transforms\fR, and \f[CR]destination\fR
fields select the drivers that make up the pipeline.
This section documents the common structure; see DRIVERS for the
settings and supported roles of each driver.
.SS BACKUP NAMES
The top\-level key of a backup definition is its canonical name.
A backup name must contain between 1 and 64 ASCII letters, digits,
underscores, or hyphens; its first character cannot be a hyphen.
The name \f[CR]settings\fR is reserved regardless of capitalization.
.PP
The optional \f[CR]previous_names\fR field is a list of earlier names
for the backup.
These names act as aliases, allowing existing backup artifacts and
configuration references to remain associated with the backup after it
is renamed.
Each previous name follows the same naming rules and must be distinct
from the canonical name and every other previous name.
.SS PIPELINE
Each backup definition requires a \f[CR]source\fR and a
\f[CR]destination\fR.
The \f[CR]source\fR selects the driver that supplies the data, and the
\f[CR]destination\fR selects the driver that stores the resulting
backup.
A source may instead refer to another configured backup, allowing its
artifacts to be copied through a new pipeline.
Yaesm validates each pipeline while reading the configuration and
rejects it if its drivers cannot exchange a compatible form of backup
data.
.PP
The optional \f[CR]transforms\fR field is a list of drivers that
process the data between the source and destination, in the order
listed.
Omitting \f[CR]transforms\fR defines a pipeline with no explicit
transformations.
Each source, transform, and destination driver is selected by a mapping
that names one driver and provides its configuration; see DRIVERS for
the available drivers and their settings.
.SS SSH
The optional \f[CR]ssh\fR field configures at most one OpenSSH
endpoint for a backup.
Each source, transform, or destination driver runs there when configured
with \f[CR]remote: true\fR and locally otherwise.
Pipelines may therefore be local\-to\-local, local\-to\-remote,
remote\-to\-local, or remote\-to\-remote on the same remote system; a
pipeline cannot directly connect two different remote systems.
.PP
The \f[CR]ssh\fR field is a mapping that requires an
\f[CR]endpoint\fR in the form \f[CR]ssh://[user\(at]host[:port]\fR.
The \f[CR]identity_file\fR and \f[CR]config_file\fR settings may
also be specified here, overriding the corresponding defaults from the
top\-level \f[CR]settings\fR section.
An \f[CR]identity_file\fR must be provided either here or in the
defaults.
.PP
Yaesm supports only non\-interactive OpenSSH public\-key authentication;
password and private\-key passphrase prompts are unavailable.
.SS SKIP UNCHANGED
The optional \f[CR]skip_unchanged\fR field is a boolean that defaults
to \f[CR]false\fR.
When enabled and an earlier artifact exists, yaesm compares the current
source with the newest artifact for the backup and skips the pipeline if
they are unchanged, regardless of which schedule created that artifact.
The destination driver must support change detection.
If yaesm cannot determine whether the source changed, it reports a
warning and creates the backup normally.
.SS SCHEDULES
The optional \f[CR]schedules\fR field is a mapping of schedule names
to schedule definitions.
Schedule names follow the same rules as backup names.
A schedule may also have a \f[CR]previous_names\fR list, which
preserves its association with artifacts created under earlier names
after the schedule is renamed.
.PP
If no on\-demand schedule is configured, yaesm implicitly adds an
on\-demand schedule named \f[CR]manual\fR that retains all of its
artifacts.
The name \f[CR]manual\fR, regardless of capitalization, may only
identify an on\-demand schedule.
Omitting \f[CR]schedules\fR therefore disables timed backups but still
allows the backup to be run manually.
.SS SCHEDULE TYPES
Each schedule definition must select exactly one of the following
schedule types.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBon\-demand\fB\fR: Defines a schedule with no timer.
Its configuration must be an empty mapping, and the schedule runs only
when explicitly selected for a manual backup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBcron\fB\fR: Runs the backup when a cron expression
matches in the scheduler time zone.
Its configuration may be the expression string directly or a mapping
containing an \f[CR]expression\fR field.
The expression contains exactly five whitespace\-separated fields in
this order: minute, hour, day of month, month, and day of week.
Fields accept \f[CR]*\fR, comma\-separated values, ranges, and
\f[CR]/\fR steps; months and weekdays may use their three\-letter
English names.
All fields must match.
Numeric weekdays run from \f[CR]0\fR for Monday through \f[CR]6\fR
for Sunday.
Cron expressions should be quoted so YAML 1.1 treats them as strings.
.SS RETENTION
Every explicitly configured schedule requires a \f[CR]retention\fR
field.
Its value may be one retention policy or a nonempty list of policies,
with each policy represented by a mapping that selects one policy type.
When multiple policies are configured, their results are combined: an
artifact is retained if any policy selects it.
Retention is applied after a backup artifact is created successfully.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkeep\-all\fB\fR: Retains every artifact created by the
schedule.
Its configuration must be an empty mapping.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkeep\-last\fB\fR: Retains the specified positive number
of newest artifacts created by the schedule.
Its configuration may be the number directly or a mapping containing a
\f[CR]count\fR field.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkeep\-for\fB\fR: Retains artifacts created within the
specified positive duration.
Its configuration may be the duration directly or a mapping containing a
\f[CR]duration\fR field.
A duration is an integer followed by \f[CR]m\fR, \f[CR]h\fR,
\f[CR]d\fR, \f[CR]w\fR, or \f[CR]y\fR for minutes, hours, days,
weeks, or 365\-day years.
.SS GROUP DEFINITIONS
Group definitions collect backups and other groups under one name so
commands can select them together.
A group is a top\-level mapping with a single \f[CR]group\fR field
containing a nonempty list of backup or group names.
Group names follow the backup naming rules and cannot duplicate a
current or previous backup name.
.PP
A group may include other groups, and definitions may appear in any
order.
Expansion preserves member order and removes duplicate backups.
Previous backup names may be used as members; unknown targets and group
cycles are configuration errors.
A group cannot be used as a backup \f[CR]source\fR.
.PP
The implicit target \f[CR]\(atall\fR selects every configured backup
in definition order and cannot be redefined.
.SS SETTINGS
The optional \f[CR]settings\fR section contains system\-wide
configuration and defaults shared by backup definitions.
Its entries are organized into subsections for the parts of yaesm they
configure.
.SS \f[CR]ssh\fR
The optional \f[CR]ssh\fR subsection supplies default OpenSSH client
settings for backup definitions.
A backup can override these defaults in its own \f[CR]ssh\fR
subsection.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBidentity_file\fB\fR: The absolute path to the private
identity file used for OpenSSH authentication.
Every backup that uses OpenSSH must specify this setting either here or
in its own \f[CR]ssh\fR subsection.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBconfig_file\fB\fR: The absolute path to an OpenSSH client
configuration file.
When specified, yaesm passes the file to OpenSSH with \f[CR]\-F\fR.
.SS \f[CR]scheduler\fR
The optional \f[CR]scheduler\fR subsection configures how scheduled
backups are run.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBtimezone\fB\fR: The time zone used to interpret backup
schedules and generate the timestamps in backup names.
The value must be an IANA time zone name.
The default is the system time zone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBmax_concurrent_backups\fB\fR: The maximum number of
backups that may run concurrently.
The value must be a positive integer.
The default is \f[CR]10\fR.
.SH DRIVERS
Drivers connect yaesm to backup tools and data formats.
A driver may supply source data, transform data, store a destination
artifact, or support more than one of these roles.
The following sections describe each driver\(aqs supported roles,
settings, and system requirements.
.SS directory
The \f[CR]directory\fR driver makes an existing local or remote
directory tree available as a backup source.
It does not create a snapshot or store backup artifacts.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]directory\fR value may be an absolute path or a mapping
containing \f[CR]location\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlocation\fB\fR: The absolute path of the directory to
read.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
As a source, the \f[CR]directory\fR driver supplies the configured
directory tree to the pipeline.
It is a live view rather than a point\-in\-time snapshot, so files may
change while the pipeline reads them.
.SS file
The \f[CR]file\fR driver makes an existing file available as a byte
stream or stores a byte stream as a backup artifact.
It may be used locally or remotely as a source or destination.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]file\fR value may be an absolute path or a mapping
containing \f[CR]location\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlocation\fB\fR: As a source, the absolute path of the
file to read.
As a destination, the absolute path of the directory beneath which
artifact files are stored.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
As a source, the \f[CR]file\fR driver reads the configured file
without modifying its contents.
It is a live view rather than a point\-in\-time snapshot, so the file
may change while the pipeline reads it.
.SS DESTINATION
As a destination, the \f[CR]file\fR driver writes the byte stream
produced by the pipeline to a new artifact file beneath
\f[CR]location\fR.
Filename suffixes contributed by the source and transformation drivers
are preserved.
Suffixes accumulate in pipeline order, so \f[CR]tar\fR followed by
\f[CR]zstd\fR and \f[CR]gpg\fR produces a filename ending in
\f[CR].tar.zst.gpg\fR.
.SS rsync
The \f[CR]rsync\fR driver uses the \f[CR]rsync\fR utility to create
and manage directory\-tree backup artifacts.
It may be used as a local or remote destination.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]rsync\fR value may be an absolute path or a mapping.
An absolute path is shorthand for a mapping containing only
\f[CR]location\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlocation\fB\fR: The absolute path of the directory
beneath which backup artifacts are stored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBexclude\fB\fR: An \f[CR]rsync\fR exclude pattern or
list of exclude patterns.
By default, no patterns are excluded.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBone_file_system\fB\fR: Whether \f[CR]rsync\fR should
avoid crossing filesystem boundaries while copying.
The default is \f[CR]false\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBextra_options\fB\fR: Additional options passed to
\f[CR]rsync\fR, given as a shell\-like string or list of shell\-like
strings.
Each string is parsed using shell quoting rules.
By default, no additional options are passed.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS DESTINATION
As a destination, the \f[CR]rsync\fR driver copies the directory tree
produced by the pipeline into a new artifact directory beneath
\f[CR]location\fR.
Copies preserve filesystem metadata, symlinks, hard links, ACLs,
extended attributes, numeric ownership, and sparse files.
.PP
When an earlier compatible \f[CR]rsync\fR artifact exists on the same
system, yaesm supplies it through \f[CR]\-\-link\-dest\fR.
Rsync then hard\-links unchanged files from that artifact instead of
storing their data again.
Every artifact remains directly browsable as a complete directory tree.
The \f[CR]rsync\fR driver does not support \f[CR]skip_unchanged\fR.
.SS btrfs
The \f[CR]btrfs\fR driver uses the \f[CR]btrfs\fR utility to back up
Btrfs subvolumes.
It can provide a subvolume as a source or store read\-only Btrfs
snapshots as a destination.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]btrfs\fR value may be an absolute path or a mapping
containing \f[CR]location\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlocation\fB\fR: As a source, the absolute path of the
Btrfs subvolume to back up.
As a destination, the absolute path of an existing directory on a Btrfs
filesystem beneath which snapshot artifacts are stored.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
As a source, the \f[CR]btrfs\fR driver takes a read\-only snapshot of
the configured subvolume.
The pipeline may read that snapshot as a directory tree or export it as
a \f[CR]btrfs send\fR stream.
Nested subvolumes are not included.
.PP
A \f[CR]file\fR destination may store the full send stream, appending
\f[CR].btrfs\fR to the artifact filename.
File artifacts are always full send streams, never incremental
\f[CR]\-p\fR streams.
.SS DESTINATION
As a destination, the \f[CR]btrfs\fR driver stores read\-only snapshot
artifacts beneath the configured location.
When the source is on the same system, yaesm first runs
\f[CR]btrfs subvolume snapshot \-r\fR.
If the source is on another system or the snapshot command exits with a
failure status, yaesm falls back to piping \f[CR]btrfs send\fR into
\f[CR]btrfs receive\fR.
.PP
When matching previous snapshots exist at both ends, the fallback uses
\f[CR]\-p\fR for an incremental transfer.
The resulting artifact is still a complete snapshot.
A \f[CR]btrfs\fR destination accepts only an unmodified Btrfs send
stream and supports \f[CR]skip_unchanged\fR.
.SS zfs
The \f[CR]zfs\fR driver uses the \f[CR]zfs\fR utility to back up ZFS
filesystem datasets.
It can provide a dataset as a source or store snapshot artifacts in a
destination dataset, using \f[CR]zfs send\fR and
\f[CR]zfs receive\fR when a transfer is required.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]zfs\fR value may be a ZFS dataset name or a mapping
containing \f[CR]dataset\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdataset\fB\fR: As a source, the ZFS filesystem dataset to
back up.
As a destination, the dataset in which snapshot artifacts are stored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBencryption\fB\fR: Whether to preserve native ZFS
encryption using raw send streams.
The source dataset must already be encrypted.
The default is \f[CR]false\fR.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
As a source, the \f[CR]zfs\fR driver takes a snapshot of the
configured dataset.
When the pipeline requires a byte stream, the snapshot is exported with
\f[CR]zfs send\fR.
Child datasets are not included.
.PP
A \f[CR]file\fR destination may store the full send stream, appending
\f[CR].zfs\fR to the artifact filename.
File artifacts are always full send streams, never incremental
\f[CR]\-i\fR streams.
.SS DESTINATION
As a destination, the \f[CR]zfs\fR driver stores snapshot artifacts in
the configured dataset.
When the source is the same dataset on the same system, yaesm creates
the snapshot directly.
Otherwise, it pipes \f[CR]zfs send\fR into \f[CR]zfs receive\fR.
.PP
When matching previous snapshots exist at both ends, the transfer uses
\f[CR]\-i\fR to send only the changes.
The resulting artifact is still a complete snapshot.
A \f[CR]zfs\fR destination accepts only an unmodified ZFS send stream
and supports \f[CR]skip_unchanged\fR.
.SS tar
The \f[CR]tar\fR driver uses the \f[CR]tar\fR utility to transform a
directory tree into an uncompressed POSIX pax archive stream.
It does not store the archive itself.
The driver works with GNU tar or bsdtar.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]tar\fR value is a mapping with the following optional
setting:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBone_file_system\fB\fR: Whether \f[CR]tar\fR should
avoid crossing filesystem boundaries while reading the directory tree.
The default is \f[CR]true\fR.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
As a transform, the \f[CR]tar\fR driver reads the directory tree
produced by the preceding driver and emits an uncompressed POSIX pax
archive stream.
Archives preserve filesystem metadata, ACLs, extended attributes, and
numeric ownership.
When the stream is stored by the \f[CR]file\fR driver, \f[CR].tar\fR
is appended to the artifact filename.
.SS zstd
The \f[CR]zstd\fR driver uses the \f[CR]zstd\fR utility to compress
a byte stream with Zstandard.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]zstd\fR value may be an integer compression level or a
mapping containing \f[CR]level\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlevel\fB\fR: An integer from \f[CR]1\fR through
\f[CR]19\fR.
Higher levels generally improve compression at the cost of more time and
processing.
The default is \f[CR]3\fR.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
As a transform, the \f[CR]zstd\fR driver compresses the byte stream
produced by the preceding driver and emits a Zstandard\-compressed
stream.
When it is stored by the \f[CR]file\fR driver, \f[CR].zst\fR is
appended to the artifact filename.
.SS gpg
The \f[CR]gpg\fR driver uses the GnuPG \f[CR]gpg\fR utility to
encrypt a byte stream with an OpenPGP public key.
.SS CONFIGURATION
The \f[CR]gpg\fR value may be the absolute path of a public\-key file
or a mapping containing \f[CR]public_key\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBpublic_key\fB\fR: The absolute path of the OpenPGP
public\-key file used to encrypt the backup.
The path is interpreted on the system where the transform runs, and the
key does not need to be imported into a GnuPG keyring.
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
As a transform, the \f[CR]gpg\fR driver encrypts the byte stream
produced by the preceding driver and emits an OpenPGP\-encrypted stream.
GnuPG\(aqs internal compression is disabled; when compression is wanted,
a compression transform such as \f[CR]zstd\fR should precede
\f[CR]gpg\fR.
When the stream is stored by the \f[CR]file\fR driver, \f[CR].gpg\fR
is appended to the artifact filename.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options must appear before the subcommand.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-h\fB, \fB\-\-help\fB\fR: Show top\-level
command\-line help and exit.
Every subcommand accepts the same option to show its own help.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-version\fB\fR: Show the yaesm version and exit.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-c FILE\fB, \fB\-\-config FILE\fB\fR: Use
\f[CR]FILE\fR as the configuration file for subcommands that read it.
The default is \f[CR]/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-log\-level LEVEL\fB\fR: Set the minimum logged
severity to \f[CR]DEBUG\fR, \f[CR]INFO\fR, \f[CR]WARNING\fR,
\f[CR]ERROR\fR, or \f[CR]CRITICAL\fR.
The default is \f[CR]INFO\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-log\-syslog[=ADDRESS]\fB\fR: Send logs to the syslog
socket at \f[CR]ADDRESS\fR.
When \f[CR]ADDRESS\fR is omitted, \f[CR]/dev/log\fR is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-log\-stderr\fB\fR: Send logs to standard error.
Standard error is used automatically when no logging destination is
selected.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-log\-file FILE\fB\fR: Append logs to \f[CR]FILE\fR.
.PP
Multiple logging destinations may be selected together.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
Where a subcommand accepts \f[CR]TARGET[,TARGET...]\fR, each target
may name a backup or group.
Groups are expanded recursively, and each selected backup is operated on
once.
The special target \f[CR]\(atall\fR selects every configured backup
and cannot be combined with other targets.
.SS backup
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
yaesm [OPTIONS] backup [\-\-schedule SCHEDULE] [\-\-control\-socket PATH] TARGET[,TARGET...]
.EE
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]backup\fR subcommand asks a running yaesm scheduler to
execute the selected backups immediately.
It streams their log messages to standard error, waits for every
selected backup to finish, and exits unsuccessfully if any backup fails.
The scheduler must have been started with \f[CR]yaesm run\fR and must
use the same control socket.
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: One or more backup or group
names, separated by commas.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-schedule SCHEDULE\fB\fR: Use the named on\-demand
schedule for every selected backup.
If omitted, each backup must have exactly one on\-demand schedule, which
yaesm selects automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-control\-socket PATH\fB\fR: Connect to the scheduler
through this Unix socket.
The default is \f[CR]/run/yaesm/control.sock\fR.
.SS check
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
yaesm [OPTIONS] check [\-\-config\-only] [\-q] [TARGET[,TARGET...]]
.EE
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]check\fR subcommand verifies that the selected backups are
ready to run.
It should be used after creating or changing the configuration, before
relying on the configured backups, and as the first step when
investigating a backup problem.
Yaesm first validates the complete configuration, then performs
read\-only checks to determine whether the selected backup pipelines can
run.
.PP
The checks depend on the drivers in each pipeline and may cover required
utilities, source and destination locations, filesystems, permissions,
encryption keys, and OpenSSH installation and connectivity.
Each check is reported as \f[CR]PASS\fR or \f[CR]FAIL\fR.
The command exits successfully only when the configuration is valid and
every performed check passes.
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: Optional backup or group names,
separated by commas.
The default is \f[CR]\(atall\fR.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-config\-only\fB\fR: Stop after validating the
configuration, without running driver checks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-q\fB, \fB\-\-quiet\fB\fR: Show only failed checks.
.SS find
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
yaesm [OPTIONS] find TARGET[,TARGET...] [QUERY...] [\-\-query QUERY...] [\-\-schedule SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]] [\-0]
.EE
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]find\fR subcommand lists existing backup artifacts for the
selected backups.
Each result is written to standard output as a destination\-specific
locator.
Results are ordered newest to oldest within each backup.
Finding no matching artifacts is successful and produces no output.
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: One or more backup or group
names, separated by commas.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBQUERY...\fB\fR: An optional query selecting artifacts by
creation time.
Omitting the query is equivalent to \f[CR]all\fR.
.SS QUERIES
Queries are applied separately to each selected backup and use one of
the following forms:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBall\fB\fR: Select every artifact.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBnewest\fB\fR: Select the newest artifact.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBoldest\fB\fR: Select the oldest artifact.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBafter TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created strictly after
\f[CR]TIME\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBbefore TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created strictly
before \f[CR]TIME\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBbetween TIME TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created at or
between the two times.
The times may be given in either order.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBclosest TIME\fB\fR: Select the artifact whose creation
time is closest to \f[CR]TIME\fR.
If two artifacts are equally close, the newer one is selected.
.SS TIME VALUES
\f[CR]TIME\fR may be a date in \f[CR]YYYY\-MM\-DD\fR form, a date
and time in \f[CR]YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM\fR form, or a time on the current
date in \f[CR]HH:MM\fR form.
A date without a time means midnight.
Date\-and\-time values may include \f[CR]Z\fR or an ISO 8601 UTC
offset; values without an offset are interpreted in the configured
scheduler time zone.
Ambiguous local times require an explicit offset, and nonexistent local
times are rejected.
.PP
A relative time has the form \f[CR]now\-Nm\fR, \f[CR]now\-Nh\fR, or
\f[CR]now\-Nd\fR, where \f[CR]N\fR is a positive decimal integer
without leading zeros and the suffix selects elapsed minutes, hours, or
days.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-q QUERY...\fB, \fB\-\-query QUERY...\fB\fR: Add
another query.
This option may be repeated; an artifact selected by any query is shown
once.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-s SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB,
\fB\-\-schedule SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB,
\fB\-\-schedules SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB\fR: Limit results to
artifacts created by the named schedules.
Current or previous schedule names may be used, names may be
comma\-separated, and the option may be repeated.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-0\fB\fR: Terminate each result with a NUL byte instead
of a newline.
.SS run
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
yaesm [OPTIONS] run [\-\-lockfile FILE] [\-\-control\-socket PATH]
.EE
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]run\fR subcommand starts the yaesm scheduler in the
foreground and runs configured timed backups until stopped.
It is generally launched and supervised by an init system.
It also creates the control socket used by \f[CR]yaesm backup\fR to
request immediate backups.
An exclusive lock prevents more than one scheduler using the same lock
file.
.SS SCHEDULING BEHAVIOR
Executions of the same backup are serialized.
An immediate execution, or an occurrence from another schedule for that
backup, waits for the current execution to finish.
If another timed occurrence of the same scheduled job becomes due while
its previous occurrence is still running, the new occurrence is skipped
and a warning is logged.
Different backups may run concurrently, subject to the configured
\f[CR]max_concurrent_backups\fR limit.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-lockfile FILE\fB\fR: Acquire the scheduler lock
through \f[CR]FILE\fR.
The default is \f[CR]/run/lock/yaesm\-run.lock\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-\-control\-socket PATH\fB\fR: Listen for control
requests on this Unix socket.
The default is \f[CR]/run/yaesm/control.sock\fR; the socket is created
with mode \f[CR]0600\fR.
.SS SIGNALS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBSIGHUP\fB\fR: Reload the configuration file selected by
\f[CR]\-\-config\fR.
A valid configuration becomes active for future scheduled and requested
backups without interrupting running backups.
If validation fails, the error is logged and the current configuration
remains active.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBSIGINT\fB, \fBSIGTERM\fB\fR: The first signal begins
a graceful shutdown.
Yaesm stops accepting backups and configuration reloads, lets running
backups finish, then closes the control socket and exits.
A second signal forcibly terminates running backup commands and causes
yaesm to exit unsuccessfully; incomplete temporary or pending artifacts
may remain.
.PP
An init system should enforce a final stop timeout and forcibly
terminate yaesm and its remaining child processes if graceful shutdown
exceeds it.
.SH FILES
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\fB\fR: Default configuration file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB/run/lock/yaesm\-run.lock\fB\fR: Default scheduler lock
file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB/run/yaesm/control.sock\fB\fR: Default Unix socket used
for control requests between \f[CR]backup\fR and \f[CR]run\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB0\fB\fR: The requested operation completed successfully.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB1\fB\fR: The requested operation failed, a check or
backup failed, yaesm encountered an unexpected error, or scheduler
shutdown was forced.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB2\fB\fR: The command line was invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB78\fB\fR: The configuration could not be read or
validated.
